put(23,'Parity observations alone do not certify quantum coherence',None,'T','Quantum information and identifiability',extline,'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which distinct states have identical parity observations, and what additional observable can distinguish their coherence?',
 'An uploaded external discussion questioned the strength of topological-qubit evidence. A finite two-qubit calculation gives the relevant identifiability principle without attributing that external discussion to the user or adjudicating device claims.',
 ['Letρ_B=|Φ⁺⟩⟨Φ⁺| with|Φ⁺⟩=(|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2, andρ_M=(|00⟩⟨00|+|11⟩⟨11|)/2.',
 'Computational-basis measurements andZZ parity are diagonal in the same basis. A coherence witness can be off diagonal, such asX⊗X.',
 'The source is uploaded external text with its own February2025discussion dates; no date of a user conversation about it is recovered.'],
 'An exact observational collision',
 'ρ_B andρ_M have identical computational-basis probabilities, identical one-qubit marginalsI/2 andZZ expectation one. ButTr(ρ_B XX)=1 andTr(ρ_M XX)=0, and their trace distance is1/2.',
 'Both states place probability one half on00 and11 and zero elsewhere. Every diagonal observable therefore has the same expectation. Partial trace removes the Bell cross terms, leaving the same marginalI/2. The operatorXX exchanges00 and11, so its expectation is the sum of those cross terms: one for the Bell state and zero for the mixture. Their difference is( |00⟩⟨11|+|11⟩⟨00| )/2, whose nonzero eigenvalues are±1/2. Its trace norm is one, giving distance one half. This proves that one class of observations can agree exactly while a different observable resolves the states.',
 'Use an equal mixture of the two states as a third candidate.',
 'It retains every stated diagonal statistic but hasXX expectation1/2. In fact the entire familyρ(q) with coherenceq/2,0≤q≤1, shares those diagonal observations whileXX readsq. Repeating only the diagonal measurements at greater precision cannot identifyq.',
 'The mathematical issue is observation sufficiency, not merely the number of measurements.',
 'This example makes no factual claim about Microsoft hardware or Majorana modes. Topological invariants, lifetimes, coherent control and error rates require different defined observations and models.',
 'Specify a measurement family and compute the kernel of its linear map on Hermitian matrices. Add observables until the targeted parameter is identifiable, then derive finite-sample confidence bounds for those observables.',[('John Watrous The Theory of Quantum Information','https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~watrous/TQI/')],origin='Uploaded external February2025topological-qubit discussion; its participants and quoted questions are not attributed to the user.')
extra={
1:' If the coefficients are time dependent but b(t) is prescribed, the integrating factor gives W(t)=e^{B(t)}[W₀+∫₀ᵗe^{−B(s)}a(s)ds], where B(t)=∫₀ᵗb(s)ds. This formula separates observable input flows from amplification and exposes exactly which missing functions a numerical forecast would need.',
2:' For the concrete network, the sum of all response rounds after round m is bounded by a geometric tail because A²=I/8. Keeping only even powers through A^{2m} leaves their scalar geometric remainder (1/8)^{m+1}/(1−1/8). Thus a finite-round approximation can carry a rigorous truncation error rather than an arbitrary stopping choice.',
3:' The solution also has a precise activation threshold: recipient i receives a positive allocation exactly when a_i>λ. As capacity grows, λ decreases and additional recipients become active. This is a mathematical consequence of the specified concave utility, not a universal conclusion about participation or social value.',
4:' With variable primary flows but fixed rates, b_t=a^t b₀+(1+g)⁻¹Σ_{j=0}^{t−1}a^{t−1−j}p_j. If 0≤a<1 and 0≤p_j≤p_max, the limiting upper bound is p_max/(g−r). This follows by summing a geometric series and makes the required bound on future flows explicit.',
5:' Dividing each income by its mean leaves the Gini unchanged, but adding the same positive constant to every income generally lowers it: the pairwise numerator stays fixed while the total-income denominator increases. This exact distinction separates scale changes from a uniform transfer and shows why both should not be called normalization.',
6:' At κ=0 the solution is A=A₀ and g=g₀−2A₀t, obtained either directly or as the continuous κ→0 limit on every bounded time interval. Substituting κ=0 into a formula containing 1/κ without taking that limit would create a spurious singularity. Zero damping is a distinct parameter case.',
8:' The exact null vector must be retained before boundary conditions are imposed. Artificially inverting the local stiffness matrix would fail because its determinant is zero. Removing a Dirichlet degree of freedom or passing to a quotient by constants changes the operator domain and can produce a positive-definite reduced system with a meaningful inverse.',
9:' In the weighted two-point example, the normalized atom is proportional to b−b_B with the opposite sign, which is precisely the equality condition in Cauchy–Schwarz. The sharp constant therefore has an explicit optimizer. If cancellation were imposed with unweighted masses instead, a constant b could already produce a nonzero weighted pairing.',
11:' For uniform Monte Carlo sampling of this four-point signal, the mean is 3/2 and the population variance is 5/4. An average of m independent samples therefore has variance 5/(4m). This sampling uncertainty is distinct from the exact Parseval identity and does not decrease a deterministic missing-mode reconstruction error.',
12:' Reusing the validation sample adaptively changes the inference problem. A final untouched test set can evaluate the selected model after selection, but its confidence statement then concerns that fixed selected model, not a fresh search over the test data. Repeatedly checking and selecting on the final set destroys this separation.',
13:' The explicit inverse maps also preserve all morphisms, not only dimensions. A linear map eM→eN induces the A-linear map reconstructed by the matrix units, and restricting that reconstruction returns the original map. This full-faithfulness check is essential: two categories can have matching object counts while having different morphism spaces.',
14:' The determinant of every monodromy matrix in this model is one because tr A_λ=0 and det e^{A_λ}=e^{tr A_λ}. Nevertheless determinant one and trace two do not force identity: the zero-potential Jordan matrix satisfies both. A geometric condition stated through traces alone must therefore address this nonsemisimple exception.',
15:' A special value can change the polynomial representation. If κ is a negative odd integer, D_κ annihilates x^{−κ} as well as the constant. This follows immediately from the odd-degree coefficient n+κ and signals an additional singular vector. Such exceptional parameters should be recorded before asserting uniform representation-theoretic behavior.',
17:' For the finite graph, removing the constant mode changes the large-time behavior completely: the remaining normalized heat trace decays as e^{−2t}, so its logarithmic derivative grows linearly in t rather than tending to zero. Zero-mode subtraction is therefore a definition-changing choice, not a harmless numerical cleanup.',
18:' The residue is stable under multiplication by any holomorphic nonvanishing factor because that factor contributes only a holomorphic logarithmic derivative. This makes zero order an invariant local quantity while the regular finite term remains normalization dependent. A proposed constant should specify which of those two pieces it intends to use.',
20:' For a commutative algebra, every commutator is zero and HH₀(A)=A itself; for k×k it has dimension two. Thus replacing M₂(k) by an algebra with the same number of displayed matrix entries or a different decomposition can change the quotient. Algebra multiplication, not vector-space size alone, determines this invariant.',
21:' At p=0 every state is fixed and there is no mixing. At p=1 every input becomes I/2 in one step. For 0<p≤1 the fixed state is unique because a fixed Bloch vector must satisfy r=(1−p)r. These endpoint and uniqueness cases are part of the exact channel theorem.'}
