put(24,'Pentagon consistency is a path identity with a norm error bound','2025-02-27','A','Coherence and operator estimates',src('2025-02-27T15:15:55Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What finite identity is tested by an associativity pentagon, and what can a tolerance of0.07 actually imply?',
 'For ordinary vector-space tensor products the five parenthesizations are canonically identified and the pentagon commutes exactly. Approximate unitary identifications accumulate error according to a stated norm, not an undefined coherence percentage.',
 ['The five parenthesizations of four ordered factors are(((AB)C)D),((A(BC))D),A((BC)D),A(B(CD)),(AB)(CD).',
 'Each associator sends a pure tensor to the same ordered four-tuple of entries, changing only brackets.',
 'For intended unitary mapsU_j and implemented unitaryV_j assume||V_j−U_j||≤ε_j in operator norm.'],
 'Exact pentagon and quantitative perturbation',
 'The two directed associator paths from(((AB)C)D) toA(B(CD)) are equal. If their edge counts are three and two, their approximate products differ by at most the sum of the five edge errors.',
 'Pure tensors span the tensor product. Along either path the elementary associators retain the same ordered entriesa,b,c,d and end at a⊗(b⊗(c⊗d)). The two linear maps are therefore equal. For approximate maps, compare each path product with its exact product using a telescoping sum. Unitary left and right factors have norm one, so one path error is at most the sum of its three edge errors and the other at most the sum of its two. Their exact middle products agree, and the triangle inequality gives the five-error bound. This proves a precise residual statement while avoiding any claim that arbitrary fusion-category matrices satisfy the pentagon.',
 'Take all five errors bounded by0.07.',
 'The worst-case path discrepancy is at most0.35. This is an operator-norm upper bound, not a statement that65%,93% or95% of operations are coherent. A much smaller observed residual may be possible through cancellation, but would require actual matrix data and an independent numerical error bound.',
 'The recovered tolerance now has a mathematical meaning tied to explicit maps and paths.',
 'Nontrivial fusion categories require their actual F-symbols, admissible labels, basis conventions and pentagon equations. Checking only that one F-matrix is unitary or involutive is insufficient. No hardware success rate follows from these algebraic identities.',
 'Given exact fusion rules, enumerate every admissible labeled pentagon and certify the maximum residual with interval arithmetic. Separate truncation error, arithmetic error and experimental error before selecting a tolerance.',[('Etingof Gelaki Nikshych and Ostrik Tensor Categories','https://math.mit.edu/~etingof/egnobookfinal.pdf')])

put(25,'A hyperbolic quotient needs an action and can have a noncompact cusp','2025-02-18','A','Hyperbolic geometry',src('2025-02-18T00:35:51Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which explicit group action defines a hyperbolic quotient, and why do finite volume and compactness remain different properties?',
 'The translation quotient of the upper half-plane gives a concrete cusp geometry with an exact area and shrinking circular sections. This supplies real quotient mathematics without deriving an automorphic economic model from notation.',
 ['LetH²={x+iy:y>0} withds²=(dx²+dy²)/y² and area formdxdy/y².',
 'The groupZ acts freely and properly discontinuously byz↦z+n. Its quotient is a smooth hyperbolic cylinder, withx periodic modulo one.',
 'The cusp regiony≥Y>0 in that quotient has a boundary circle aty=Y and remains unbounded in the logarithmic radial direction.'],
 'Exact cusp area and noncompactness',
 'The cusp area is1/Y and its horizontal section aty has length1/y. It is noncompact despite finite area. The entire translation quotient has infinite area.',
 'Translation preservesy anddx, so it preserves the metric. For any compact subset ofH² only finitely many integer translates meet it, proving proper discontinuity; no nonzero translation fixes a point. A strip0≤x<1 is a fundamental domain. Integrating its cusp area gives∫₀¹dx∫_Y^∞y⁻²dy=1/Y. The circle at heighty has length∫₀¹dx/y=1/y. Along a vertical path the distance fromY toy islog(y/Y), which tends to infinity, so no compact set contains the whole cusp. Extending the area integral down toy=0 diverges, showing that the full quotient and its cusp subregion must not be confused.',
 'SetY=2 and compare sections aty=2 andy=8.',
 'The cusp area is1/2. Its two section lengths are1/2 and1/8, while the vertical separation islog4. The cross sections shrink but recede to infinite distance, explaining finite area without compactness.',
 'Quotient, metric, measure and global geometry are all specified independently of an application label.',
 'A quotient[H³/Γ] requires an actual group, action and a choice between an orbifold/stack and a smooth manifold. Automorphic functions and Hecke operators need additional arithmetic structure; arbitrary urban data do not supply it.',
 'For a supplied discrete arithmetic group, determine stabilizers, fundamental-domain volume and cusp structure. Then formulate the exact function space and Laplacian domain before invoking a discrete spectral expansion or trace formula.',[('Peter Buser Geometry and Spectra of Compact Riemann Surfaces','https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-8176-4992-0')])
